# ---------------------------------------------------------------- making peace with it (cross cycle s2)
# What the attempt gave: cheering someone younger (W), teaching (U), the story told well (B), the local players at last
# (R, an entry rung open to anyone: [amateur actor], the start of the road back to the lead), letting it rest (G); the
# cross cycle at 78 is thanks, a play for the youth theatre, a small fund, the story told to the youth theatre, and the
# lesson written down. Here the anti-story pays off: the peace reading and the Book of Moments can name the long shot.

== the long shot becomes a story to tell
tier: echo
rate: 0.15
once: yes
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-100
times: per person who missed a stage long shot: about 1 in 5 come to tell it as a story of their own, years later, in a class, at a table or to someone younger (estimate)
tone: joy
life: meaning, friends, performing
horizon: years
roles: friend, partner, child, colleague
earth: years after the long shot that missed, someone younger asks about it, and the story is there to be told
tribal: winters after the attempt that failed, a child at the fire asks about it, and the telling is there
magic: years after the attempt that failed, an apprentice asks about it in the tavern, and the tale is there to be told
after: a long shot missed in one of the moments of stage-longshots.lib (as for 'the same door, years later'), holding [a long shot that missed]; now 25 or more and not playing a lead (no [lead actor or actress])
delay: 5-30
likelier: years gone by since the attempt; teaching, telling stories or acting kept up; much White and Green in the color history
rarer: stress above 1.5; poor health
scene: Years after the long shot that missed, a young cousin who has just joined the youth theatre asks {N} at a family lunch whether it is true that {N} once went for something enormous and did not get it. The whole table goes quiet to hear the answer.
scene W: {N} tells it straight, the way it happened: what was asked, what {N} did, and the letter that said no. There is nothing in it to be ashamed of, and the young ones should know that.
scene U: Over the years {N} has understood the day better than at the time: what the people in the room were looking for, and what {N} had not yet learned. That is worth passing on.
scene B: It has become a good story, and {N} knows it. It gets a laugh in the right place, and people remember who told it.
scene R: The ache has gone, mostly, and what is left is the memory of the nerve: the morning {N} walked in anyway. {N} would not trade it for anything.
scene G: It belongs to the family's stories now, somewhere between the grandmother's dance-hall days and the uncle's famous fish. {N} likes it there.
scene tribal: At the fire one winter a child asks {N} whether it is true that {N} once walked up to the elders and asked for the great mask, and did not get it. The whole hearth goes quiet to hear the telling.
scene magic: In the tavern one evening a young apprentice asks {N} whether it is true that {N} once went before the master of revels for the hero's part. The table goes quiet to hear the tale.
